\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Stokes' theorem for a cell subcomplex}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring and let #{X} be the torus
of \ref{fdip-00NZ}, with #{N\ge 3}. A \newvocab{cell subcomplex}
is a set of cubes, of any dimensions, closed under taking faces,
each cube carrying the coordinate orientation of that torus. Let
#{M} be the chain which sums the maximal cubes of such a set, and
let #{\partial M} be its cellular boundary. For a
form #{\omega} of the matching degree, integration over a chain is
the evaluation pairing of \ref{fdip-00NZ}. Then
##{\int_M d\omega=\int_{\partial M}\omega.}
On a directed cycle of length at least #{3}, an arc of edges from
#{a} to #{b} has boundary the endpoints #{+b} and #{-a}, and the
identity is the telescoping sum
##{\sum_{i=a}^{b-1}\bigl(f(i+1)-f(i)\bigr)=f(b)-f(a).}}

\proof{
\p{The isomorphism of \ref{fdip-00NZ} identifies #{d} with the
transpose of the cellular boundary, in the sense that
#{\langle d\omega,c\rangle=\langle\omega,\partial c\rangle} for
every chain #{c}. The case #{c=M} is
#{\int_M d\omega=\int_{\partial M}\omega}.}

\p{On the cycle the same pairing sums the forward differences along
the arc. Consecutive terms cancel, and the two endpoints remain.}
}
